Title: The fold sweep and the keyword drivers measure a tokenizer upstream no longer ships
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T12:07Z claude created

--- body ---

## Context

Filed by the v1.13.0 re-baseline (ticket 0618), which found it rather than
looked for it.

Upstream's keyword substrate moved in v1.13.0. `passages_fts` used to be
declared `unicode61 remove_diacritics 2`, which folded accents on the way in;
it is now `remove_diacritics 0`, which keeps them, and the unaccented spelling
is reached query-side by expanding a term into the accented spellings the
index actually holds — but only when their summed document frequency exceeds
the typed spelling's own, and only in that one direction. An accented query
never expands. That change is this repository's own pull request #46.

Seven places in this tree still declare `remove_diacritics 2`, and each of them
either produced a committed artifact or would produce the next one:

- `bench/fold_sweep.mjs` — the R19 gate of SPEC.md §5.2.8, in four places
  including the probe table it builds and the tokenizer string it stamps into
  its own artifact.
- `bench/fts5_keyword_arm.mjs`, `bench/constrained_match.mjs`,
  `bench/fts5_bench.mjs` — keyword measurement drivers that build their own
  synthetic corpora.
- `verification/probes/droplist-adapts-to-corpus.mjs`,
  `keeping-diacritics-cost.mjs`, `folding-cost-and-benefit.mjs`,
  `diacritic-collision-cost.mjs` — probes whose findings are cited in prose.

## Why this is a re-measurement and not an edit

Changing the declaration would change what these drivers measure, so it cannot
ride a re-baseline: a re-baseline moves a pin and re-reads claims, it does not
silently replace a number. `bench/results/0578-fold-sweep/codepoints.json` is a
dated record of a red verdict against a tokenizer upstream shipped at the time,
and it stays. What must change is that nothing may cite it as a fact about
*current* upstream until the sweep has been re-run against the tokenizer
upstream now ships. `README.md`'s R19 row already says so.

The two-sided asymmetry is the substance, not the flag value. The old gate
asked one question — do our two normalizers agree — because both sides folded.
The new arrangement has no symmetric question to ask: the index side folds
nothing, the query side folds only to look up variants, and whether a user's
query finds the document is decided by the dominance gate and the variant cap.
A sweep re-run with the flag flipped and the same probe table would answer a
question nobody has.

## Relevant files

- `bench/fold_sweep.mjs` — the gate; `make fold-gate`; exit codes 0/1/2/3.
- `bench/results/0578-fold-sweep/codepoints.json` — the dated red. Do not rewrite.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8 — the gate's contract, which says the gate runs green by
  right against current upstream; `DECISIONS.md` already carries an unratified
  entry saying that sentence and the measured red cannot both stand, and the
  substrate change makes that entry's subject a third thing again.
- `bench/index_schema.mjs` — the header now records the consequence for drivers.

## Actions

1. Decide what the gate asks now, given the asymmetry. That is a design
   question and it comes before any code.
2. Re-run whatever the answer is against a built checkout at the reviewed
   baseline, into a NEW artifact directory.
3. Re-state SPEC.md §5.2.8 and the R19 row from what the new run found.
4. Sweep the four measurement drivers and the four probes: for each, either
   re-measure or record in its own header that its artifact is history.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] No file in `bench/` or `verification/probes/` declares the old tokenizer
      without a line saying why it is measuring history
- [ ] `README.md`'s R19 row cites an artifact measured against the tokenizer
      upstream ships at the reviewed baseline, or says plainly that none exists
- [ ] The `DECISIONS.md` entry on the gate's colour is answerable against one
      substrate rather than two
